**Official solution:** the algorithm finds $r$ with $a^r\equiv1$ and hopes $a^{r/2}-1$ and $a^{r/2}+1$ each contain a different factor of $N$. But every factor of $p^t$ is a power of $p$. If both are multiples of $p$, so is their difference, which is 2. So it can only work when $p = 2$, and powers of 2 don't need a quantum computer. In fact $a^{r/2}$ is always $\pm1$, because 1 has only two square roots mod $p^t$, though proving that takes more number theory than the course expects.

**Notes:** order finding works fine, but the reduction fails. For odd $p$, $(\mathbb{Z}/p^t)^*$ is cyclic, so $x^2\equiv1 \pmod{p^t}$ has only the solutions $\pm1$. If $r$ is even, then $a^{r/2}
ot\equiv1$ (by minimality of $r$) forces $a^{r/2}\equiv -1$. So $\gcd(a^{r/2}\pm1, N)$ is always trivial.
